= Priming =

== Overview ==
'''Priming''' is a change in later processing, response, or accessibility caused by prior exposure to a stimulus or related information.

Priming is observed in multiple experimental families, including repetition priming, perceptual priming, and semantic priming.

== Repetition priming ==
Repeated presentation of an item can make later identification or response faster or more accurate.

This can occur even when explicit recollection is weak, showing that priming and conscious memory are not identical.

== Semantic priming ==
A target can be processed faster after a semantically related prime.

For example, a word related to the target may reduce lexical-decision response time.

Semantic priming is often used to study lexical organization and spreading or predictive activation.

== Perceptual priming ==
Prior exposure can facilitate later processing of perceptual form.

The effect can depend on modality, visual form, viewpoint, and task.

== Priming and awareness ==
Some priming effects occur when participants are not deliberately using the prime.

That does not mean all priming is unconscious or that unnoticed cues can reliably control complex behavior.

== Social and behavioral priming ==
Claims that subtle primes produce large downstream effects on complex social behavior have had a more mixed replication record than classic perceptual, repetition, and semantic priming.

These literatures should not be collapsed into one evidentiary claim.

== Priming and persuasion ==
Priming changes accessibility or processing.

Persuasion changes attitudes, beliefs, or choices through broader communicative mechanisms.

A priming effect does not by itself demonstrate persuasion or mind control.

== Related concepts ==
[[Memory]], [[Attention]], [[Salience]], and [[Semantic similarity]] are closely related but distinct mechanisms or measurements relevant to priming.

